          <strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for educational purposes only. Cardiovascular risk assessment requires a doctor's evaluation combining test results with your age, blood pressure, smoking history, and other factors.
        </div>

        <h2>What Is a Lipid Profile Test?</h2>
        <p>A <strong>lipid profile</strong> (also called a lipid panel or cholesterol test) measures the fats (lipids) in your blood. It is typically done after 9–12 hours of fasting and reports the following key parameters:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Total Cholesterol</li>
          <li>LDL Cholesterol (Low-Density Lipoprotein) — "bad" cholesterol</li>
          <li>HDL Cholesterol (High-Density Lipoprotein) — "good" cholesterol</li>
          <li>Triglycerides</li>
          <li>VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein)</li>
          <li>Non-HDL Cholesterol</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Indians are genetically predisposed to higher triglycerides and lower HDL compared to Western populations, making lipid testing particularly important for South Asians.</p>

        <h2>Lipid Profile Normal Range (India — ICMR / AHA Guidelines)</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="text-sm w-full border border-hairline rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-canvas-soft">
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">Parameter</th>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">Desirable</th>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">Borderline</th>
                <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold text-primary border-b border-hairline">High Risk</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">Total Cholesterol</td><td class="p-3 text-green-700">&lt; 200 mg/dL</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600">200–239</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600">≥ 240</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">LDL Cholesterol</td><td class="p-3 text-green-700">&lt; 100 mg/dL</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600">130–159</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600">≥ 160</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">HDL Cholesterol (Men)</td><td class="p-3 text-green-700">≥ 40 mg/dL</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600">35–40</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600">&lt; 35</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">HDL Cholesterol (Women)</td><td class="p-3 text-green-700">≥ 50 mg/dL</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600">45–50</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600">&lt; 45</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">Triglycerides</td><td class="p-3 text-green-700">&lt; 150 mg/dL</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600">150–199</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600">≥ 200</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium">Non-HDL Cholesterol</td><td class="p-3 text-green-700">&lt; 130 mg/dL</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-600">130–159</td><td class="p-3 text-red-600">≥ 160</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>LDL — The "Bad" Cholesterol</h2>
        <p>LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein) carries cholesterol from the liver to cells. When LDL is too high, excess cholesterol deposits in artery walls as plaque, progressively narrowing arteries. This is called atherosclerosis and is the main cause of heart attacks and strokes.</p>
        <p><strong>Target LDL levels vary by risk:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Low risk (no diabetes, no prior heart disease): LDL &lt; 130 mg/dL</li>
          <li>Moderate risk (1–2 risk factors): LDL &lt; 100 mg/dL</li>
          <li>High risk (diabetes, prior heart attack, or multiple risk factors): LDL &lt; 70 mg/dL</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>HDL — The "Good" Cholesterol</h2>
        <p>HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein) works in reverse — it picks up excess cholesterol from arteries and brings it back to the liver for removal. <strong>Higher HDL is better</strong> and is protective against heart disease.</p>
        <p>Indians typically have genetically lower HDL levels. Exercise is the most effective way to raise HDL — even a 30-minute brisk walk most days of the week can increase it by 5–10%.</p>

        <h2>Triglycerides</h2>
        <p>Triglycerides are the most common type of fat in the body, stored in fat cells and released as energy between meals. High triglycerides are strongly linked to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Excess refined carbohydrates and sugar in diet (maida, sugar, white rice in excess)</li>
          <li>Excess alcohol consumption</li>
          <li>Uncontrolled diabetes</li>
          <li>Hypothyroidism</li>
          <li>Obesity</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fasting triglycerides above 500 mg/dL carry a risk of pancreatitis and require urgent treatment.</p>

        <h2>Indian Diet Changes to Improve Your Lipid Profile</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ <strong>Use mustard oil or olive oil</strong> for cooking (avoid vanaspati/dalda which contains trans fats)</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Eat more oats and barley</strong> — beta-glucan in these grains is proven to lower LDL</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Add walnuts (akhrot) and flaxseeds (alsi)</strong> — rich in omega-3 fatty acids</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Eat more dal and legumes</strong> — soluble fiber binds cholesterol in the gut</li>
          <li>✅ <strong>Use garlic and onion liberally</strong> — both have modest cholesterol-lowering effects</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Avoid:</strong> Ghee in excess, full-fat cream, red meat, fried snacks (pakoras, samosas, chips), and coconut oil in large amounts</li>
          <li>❌ <strong>Limit refined carbs:</strong> White rice, maida, sugar — these raise triglycerides significantly</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="border border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 rounded-lg p-6 my-8 text-center">
